✅   What is the role of ingress ? 

Exposes services outside the cluster using HTTP or HTTPS 
Route incoming traffic  using host based / path based routing . 
Supports SSL termination 
Offers load balancing  for better traffic management 

We can manage access to multiple services through a single external IP 

To overcome limitations of Load Balancer.
For NodePort, we cannot expose the node IP to the internet. nodePort usually is used for within organization and its not good to expose to external customers.
 
Problems with Kubernetes Services -
1. It is dependent on  cloud provider. It asks Cloud provider to create LB for svc where load balancer is available in  static public IP.
2. Security issue - We don’t have control , we are very much dependent on cloud provider. We have to use what is provided.
Services in k8s was not providing Enterprise level load balancing  capabilities. such as path based / host based / whitelisting / black listing / sticky sessions / load based routing/ Ratio based load balancing  etc & TLS loadBalancer
    3. Enterprise and TLS Load Balancing Capabilities - Services in K8s was missing Enterprise level Load Balancing capabilities such as sticky session, host based LB , path based , whitelisting / black listing, ratio based LB
    4. Cloud Provider charges for each services. Charged for each static public IP. Hence, Load Balancer Service is expensive.    
Ingress comes in to picture -
1. Ingress defines routing.  We can perform host based / path based routing, cookie based routing, whitelisting ips , backlisting ips etc
2. Ingress can use a single IP address handling all services.
3.  Ingress solves the above problem of enterprise load balancing capabilities
 What Ingress Does?   
* User will Create ingress resource defining type of rules they need. They will also decide the type of load balancer they need to use such as f5, nignx etc
* LB companies  like nginx / F5 etc will write their ingress controller and share steps to install in github etc
* As a user, we need to deploy ingress controller using helm etc based on requirement.


✅  What is difference between stateful and stateless applications in Kubernetes , How are stateful sets and Deployments used to manage them ? 

Stateless application   - Don’t store data between sessions Every pod is identical and replaceable , these are managed through deployments which handles scaling and rolling updates . Used for web application like  redis

Stateful application - are those that stores / retain data . They are managed using stateful sets.  We can predict the pod name & order and network  identity. The pod no. starts from 0 to N-1 . N is no. of replicas Used for mysql , kafkaesque, redid 
pod will be created with the help of  ordinal index  . starts from 0 to N-1 [ N = number of replicas ]
Note - Stateful  set needs headless service  Headless Service - is a service that is neither  exposed internally nor externally  just that it is available on a port ,but is a cluster ip type & clusterIp should be none.


Key characteristics:
* Requires persistent storage
* Each pod has a unique identity
* Order of startup/shutdown may matter
* Data must survive pod restarts

✅   What Happens when you run k apply -f deploy.yaml ?  
User send a request to deploy  your application to k8s  through kubectl or yaml 

The api server processes the requests performs authentication , authorization  and validation  &  stores it  in etcd in  key:value format

The scheduler picks a worker node with enough resources by checking cpu/memory , Taints toleration  , Affinity / Anti Affinity 

The Kubelet on the worker node checks for the pod specs , informs the container runtime , Container runtime pulls the images invokes the network plugin under /opt/cni/bin & starts the container 

The kube-proxy ensure the app is accessible  via networking rule

Controller keep monitoring the system and make fixes if needed 
  ✅   What if the scheduler is down? Will k8s still create new pods?
No. Without a scheduler , new pods cannot get scheduled on any nodes. Existing pods will continue  to run, however autoscaling & new deployments will not work    ✅ What happens if etcd  crashes?  
K8s can’t read / write cluster state anymore. Cluster becomes unstable . Control plane components will not work properly 

✅ Can a Pod be scheduled without a kubelet on the node? 
No,  kubelet is responsible for running / managing the pod . If kubelet is missing, we cannot schedule pods 

✅ Role of kube  proxy 
In parallel, every node also runs a Kube Proxy, which monitors cluster changes via the API Server and sets up forwarding rules whenever a new service is created.  It is a traffic director in k8s . It takes care of service to pod routing , Kube-proxy maintains routing table  Performs load distribution - which pod should receive traffic 
performs health check - ensures traffic is routed to healthy pod 

