✅  How does K8s handle persistent storage for containers ? 

K8s uses PVs and PVCs to manage storage for stateful applications 

PV is a piece of storage in a cluster provisioned by an admin or dynamically . The PV exists independently of the pod.  
PVC - PVC is basically request for storage.  request by a pod for storage specifying size and access mode 


Binding b/n pv and pvc happens based on size, access mode and storage class . Once bound ,  pac will always point to the pv. 



✅  Static Provisioning 
Manually Create Storage before any application asks for it 


As a cluster Admin , We create a pv  When someone requests for a storage with certain size and access mode , if k8s fins a relevant PV , the PVC will be bound to the PV. 
Its the pod there the PVC is defined 


✅  Dynamic  Provisioning 
It removes the manual creation of PV 
Instead of an admin pre-creating PVs , K8s creates the Storage when an application asks for it. It happens through storage class 


When a PVC references a storage class , k8s talks to the stage backend and creates the pv on the fly. The pv is immediately bound to the PVC . The storage is created only when it is needed. 

✅  Access Mode

RWO [ ReadWrite Once ] - The volume can be mounted as RW  by only one node at a time  . All pods running on the same node can access the data .. 
RWX [ Read Write Many ] - The volume can be mounted as RW  by multiple  nodes at the same time

✅  Reclaim policy 
What happens when pod is deleted ? Nothing happens  to storage data when pod is deleted. 
When pac is deleted , We need to decide what will happen to the data 
Retain -  if  pvc is deleted , pv will be release. Data will be kept as it is. K8s doesn’t delete the data and doesn’t make the volume available for automatic reuse 
Delete  - if  pvc is deleted , pv will be deleted , Data too  is gone 

